# The base problems of the CSES families repeated_cses_coin_combinations_ordered_*: a few coin values and a sum x, the answer the number of
# ordered ways, two sequences of coins that differ in order being different ways, modulo 10^9 + 7. One row has coins
# that permute (4, 1, 3), where the unordered count differs, and two have no way at all.
#
# THE ANSWER of each row is the problem's definition computed exhaustively by a
# brute-force script (2026-11-21), which enumerates every candidate the problem's
# statement describes. PROOF: the program of the problem's file follows the
# definition, and its header argues under Definition 14 that its one normal form
# is that answer; the data are finite and within the family's bottom rungs, which
# the reference reading answers. The inputs are the author's own, no CSES test
# data.
#
#   coin_combinations_ordered_point(?Point, ?Coins, ?Sum, ?Result)
#
# Coins is a Scott list (corpora/library/prelude/list) of integers and Sum an integer, each from_cbor of an integer
# literal (corpora/library/integer/cbor); Result is such an integer, the count modulo 10^9 + 7 (every count here is
# below the modulus, so the reduction is not exercised: the enumeration the definition asks for cannot reach a count
# above 10^9 + 7).
